  <h2 class="project-section-title">Challenges</h2>
  <p>
    The goal is to estimate contact locations ($\mathbf{r}_{c,i} \in \mathbb{R}^3$) and forces ($\mathbf{F}_{ext,i} \in \mathbb{R}^3$) using
    proprioceptive sensors, the joint torque sensors (JTSs) and a base F/T sensor. The external joint torque and the wrench at the base caused by the
    contact forces are
  </p>
  <div aria-label="Equation 1" class="project-equation" role="region" tabindex="0">
    $$ \hat{\mathbf{W}} = \begin{bmatrix} \hat{\boldsymbol{\tau}}_{ext} \\ \hat{\mathbf{W}}_{ext,b} \end{bmatrix}, \qquad \boldsymbol{\tau}_{ext} =
    \sum_{i=1}^{k} \mathbf{J}_i^{\top}(\mathbf{q}, \mathbf{r}_{c,i})\,\mathbf{F}_{ext,i}, \qquad \mathbf{W}_{ext,b} = \sum_{i=1}^{k}
    \underbrace{\begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{I}_{3\times3} \\ \mathrm{skew}(\mathbf{r}_{c,i}) \end{bmatrix}}_{\mathbf{X}_{c,i}(\mathbf{r}_{c,i})}
    \mathbf{F}_{ext,i} $$
  </div>
  <p>
    Finding the contact locations and forces that best explain $\hat{\mathbf{W}}$ is a nonlinear optimization problem, since $\mathbf{r}_c$ and
    $\mathbf{F}_c$ appear as a cross product. Particle filter (PF)-based approaches have been proposed for this problem, but two challenges remain:
  </p>
  <ul class="project-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Run-time:</strong> the run-time is typically slow, mainly due to the computation time in the motion model of the PF (e.g., about 10 Hz
      for a 30-DOF humanoid robot, and about 200 Hz for a 7-DOF robot arm).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Singularity:</strong> multiple solutions that explain all sensor measurements may exist. Such singularities may appear quite frequently
      when only JTSs are considered. For example, if a contact is on link 1 and the robot is equipped with only JTSs, the sensing DOF is 1, and the
      contact force and location cannot be estimated.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2 class="project-section-title">Key idea: Multi-Contact Particle Filter with Exploration Particles (MCP-EP)</h2>
  <ul class="project-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Run-time:</strong> we improve the run-time of the PF with a mesh preprocessing that computes mesh data offline (e.g., distance between
      all face pairs). This can only be done for each link separately, and consequently, particles cannot be updated across links. Therefore, we
      modified the PF by introducing exploration particles, which are forced to investigate adjacent links.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Singularity:</strong> we mitigate the singularity by introducing an F/T sensor at the base of the robot. With this setup, we can
      estimate a contact at any link including links 1 and 2, and can estimate multiple contacts even for a collaborative robot which has only a few
      DOF.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2 class="project-section-title">Technical components</h2>
  <h3 class="project-subsection-title">1. QP-based contact force identification</h3>
  <div class="project-grid project-grid-2">
    <div class="project-callout-box">
      <p>
        Assuming a point contact, the $i$th contact force is constrained to lie inside a friction cone, which is approximated as a polyhedral cone,
        $\mathbf{F}_{c,i} = \mathbf{A}_{c,i}\mathbf{f}_{c,i}$. If, in addition, $\mathbf{r}_c$ is fixed, the problem becomes a convex QP in which the
        contact force is the only decision variable:
      </p>
      <div aria-label="Equation 2" class="project-equation" role="region" tabindex="0">
        $$\min_{\mathbf{f}_c} \big\| \hat{\mathbf{W}} - \mathbf{Q}^{\top}\mathbf{f}_c \big\|^2 \;\; \text{s.t. } f_{c,i,1}, \ldots, f_{c,i,4} \le 0$$
      </div>
      <p>By virtue of the base F/T sensor, 6 sensing DOFs are added to the QP regardless of the contact location.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="project-media-card">
      <a aria-label="Open full image: Polyhedral friction cone" href="{{ '/assets/img/research/mcpep/fig_friction_cone.png' | relative_url }}"
        ><img
          alt="Polyhedral friction cone"
          decoding="async"
          loading="lazy"
          src="{{ '/assets/img/research/mcpep/fig_friction_cone.png' | relative_url }}"
      ></a>
      <div class="card-caption">
        <strong>Point contact assumption</strong> realized by the friction cone formulation, approximated as a polyhedral cone with support vectors
        $\mathbf{a}_{c,i,j}$.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3 class="project-subsection-title">2. Motion-model with mesh preprocessing</h3>
  <div class="project-grid project-grid-2">
    <div class="project-media-card">
      <a aria-label="Open full image: Mesh preprocessing" href="{{ '/assets/img/research/mcpep/preprocessing.webp' | relative_url }}"
        ><img alt="Mesh preprocessing" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="{{ '/assets/img/research/mcpep/preprocessing.webp' | relative_url }}"
      ></a>
      <div class="card-caption">
        <strong>Mesh preprocessing.</strong> Non-contact parts are eliminated, and an isotropic remesh algorithm makes the size of the faces almost
        uniform. The position of a particle is represented using the link and face index number, <code>(link#, face#)</code>.
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="project-media-card">
      <video
        aria-label="Motion-model with preprocessed mesh data. The geodesic distances for all face pairs are computed offline and stored in ascending order. Using this data structure, the random walk is applied simply by changing the face index of a particle (about 0.06 µs to update one particle)."
        controls=""
